-- Migration 18: the comments of the derived relations and of case_meta describe the current definitions (comments only;
-- no schema or data change). Migration 15's cloud_services comment said "No application name or id is involved", wrong
-- since first-party applications are excluded by id; case_meta now also holds the profile's stored lists.

COMMENT ON TABLE cloud_services IS 'Tenant-wide cloud services per tenant, rewritten by every profile run (after spray_sources), one row per (tenant, application id, hosting ASN), established from the WHOLE case (no prefix: a capture is a window). A pair is a service when at least 5 distinct users have known successes (error_code 0, interactive or not) to the application from that hosting ASN (ip_intel.is_hosting) on at least 3 distinct UTC days AND those sign-ins are at least 80% of the application''s known successes (an unknown result never helps). It is NOT a service when (a) sprayed users (a credential failure, interactive or of unknown kind, from a spray source of the tenant) are at least 20% of its users or at least 3 of them, or (b) at least 3 distinct users have an interactive credential failure to the application from that ASN (the credential failure codes are the credential_failure_codes list, stored in case_meta). A Microsoft first-party application (the microsoft_first_party_apps list, stored in case_meta) is never a service, whatever its users or days. Typical case: a third-party SaaS connector whose public-cloud backend signs users in on their behalf (the portal export records those as interactive). SPW-ID-002 and SPW-ID-003 excuse every sign-in, and every UAL UserLoggedIn carrying the application id (ual_events.app_id), of that application from that same ASN; a sign-in from a spray source always counts. Residual: up to 2 sprayed users (under 20%), up to 2 failing users, 5+ phished accounts on one VPS with no failures, and an attacker using the same application from the connector''s own ASN are excused.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN cloud_services.tenant_key IS 'The tenant (tenant.tenant_key). The rule scope shows a rule only its own tenant''s rows.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN cloud_services.app_id IS 'The application id, lower-case, as signins.app_id stores it. Never an id of the microsoft_first_party_apps list.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN cloud_services.asn IS 'The hosting ASN (ip_intel.asn, is_hosting) the service signs in from; with app_id the key of the service.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN cloud_services.hosting_users IS 'Distinct users (lower-cased UPN) with a known success to the application from this ASN in the case (at least 5).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN cloud_services.hosting_days IS 'Distinct UTC days with a known success to the application from this ASN in the case (at least 3).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN cloud_services.signins IS 'Known successful sign-ins to the application (any address) in the case.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN cloud_services.hosting_signins IS 'Known successful sign-ins to the application from this ASN in the case (at least 80% of signins).';

COMMENT ON TABLE spray_sources IS 'Password-spray source addresses per tenant, rewritten by every profile run, before cloud_services. For each (address, user) with an interactive credential failure from the address (a code of the credential_failure_codes list, stored in case_meta; a sign-in of unknown kind counts as interactive), the user is EXCUSED only if they have a known success (error_code 0) from that address either BEFORE their first credential failure there (the address was already theirs) or within 30 minutes AFTER one of their failures there (a typo correction). Every other failed user is a SPRAYED user, including users whose only successes there come later (the takeover pattern). An address is a source when at least 5 users were sprayed AND the sprayed users outnumber the excusing users (distinct users with a known success from the address, minus the late-takeover users). An office NAT or VPN egress, where users mistype and succeed within minutes or have signed in for days, is not a source; a proxy that sprays and takes accounts over stays one. The address type does not matter and no GeoIP is needed. Rules read it through the tenant scope like any tenant-aware table; cloud_services reads it to disqualify a pair whose users were sprayed.';

COMMENT ON TABLE case_meta IS 'Case-level constants and profile state, one row per key. case_id: random UUID set by migration 16, the audit chain genesis. audit_head: the head (seq and hash) the last audit insert left. post_migration_pending: present while a migrated case still owes its post-migration step; the value is the schema version before the migration. credential_failure_codes: JSON array of integers, the codes the last profile run derived spray_sources and cloud_services with. first_party_apps: JSON array of lower-case application ids, the microsoft_first_party_apps list the last profile run built cloud_services with. An upgrade reuses the two stored lists, so it never swaps a case''s custom lists for the environment''s.';
COMMENT ON COLUMN case_meta.key IS 'Constant name (case_id, audit_head, post_migration_pending, credential_failure_codes, first_party_apps).';
COMMENT ON COLUMN case_meta.value IS 'Constant value: text, or JSON for the stored lists.';
